<main>
  <section class="ch" id="define">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <h2>Define the tickets <em>with</em> Agent Smith</h2>
        <p>Work it out is a conversation with an agent that reads your repositories while it answers. It splits the change into tickets in the order they can merge, names what each one requires, and files nothing until you approve.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ch-body">
        <div>
          <div class="example-label">Example · a payment method across two repos</div>
          <div class="app">
            <div class="app-bar"><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="path">Work it out · acme-shop</span><span class="tag working">reading code</span></div>
            <div class="app-body">
              <div class="msg"><div class="av" aria-hidden="true">HL</div><div><div class="who">you</div><div class="bubble"><p>Customers in Germany keep asking for SEPA direct debit at checkout. What would that take?</p></div></div></div>
              <div class="msg"><div class="av av-smith" aria-hidden="true">AS</div><div>
                <div class="who">agent smith</div>
                <div class="reads"><span>acme-api/Checkout/PaymentProviderRegistry.cs</span><span>acme-web/src/checkout/PaymentStep.tsx</span><span>acme-api/Billing/Mandates/</span></div>
                <div class="bubble">
                  <p>Payment providers register in <code class="c">PaymentProviderRegistry</code>, so the provider itself is small. There is no place to store a signed mandate yet, and the payment step has no IBAN field. I would cut it into three tickets:</p>
                  <div class="proposal">
                    <div class="tk"><span class="n">1</span><div><div class="t">Store SEPA mandates with signature date</div><div class="r">acme-api · requires nothing</div></div><span class="tag">4 criteria</span></div>
                    <div class="tk"><span class="n">2</span><div><div class="t">SEPA provider in the payment registry</div><div class="r">acme-api · requires 1</div></div><span class="tag">5 criteria</span></div>
                    <div class="tk"><span class="n">3</span><div><div class="t">IBAN entry and mandate text in checkout</div><div class="r">acme-web · requires 2</div></div><span class="tag">6 criteria</span></div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="approve"><span class="btn btn-go btn-sm">File 3 work items in Azure Boards</span><span>Nothing is filed until you click.</span></div>
                </div>
              </div></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="notes">
          <div><h3>It reads before it proposes</h3><p>While it works you see which files it opens, and every fact in a proposal cites its file and line.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Tickets in merge order</h3><p>Each proposed ticket says which one it requires. Agent Smith works them in that order, so ticket 3 never builds on code that ticket 2 hasn't merged.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Drop in what you have</h3><p>Upload a screenshot, a PDF or a spec. Paste a URL, or a Figma link once Figma is connected. Uploads stay with the conversation.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ch" id="run">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <h2>One ticket, <em>every</em> repo, one pull request each</h2>
        <p>This is the factory floor. The approved ticket becomes a series of specs. Fresh reviewers check each spec before anything is built. Then every repository gets its own sandbox with its own toolchain, and every acceptance criterion is checked against evidence before a pull request is marked ready for review.</p>
      </div>

      {% include "partials/lifecycle-stage.njk" %}

      <div class="steps">
        <div><b>Ticket</b>A work item arrives by service hook, polling or a tag, or straight from Work it out.</div>
        <div><b>Specs</b>The ticket becomes a series of specs, and each one is attacked by fresh reviewers before any code exists.</div>
        <div><b>Build</b>Every repository gets its own sandbox and toolchain. Specs run in their required order.</div>
        <div><b>Verify</b>Each criterion is met, unmet or not applicable, with the evidence that decided it.</div>
        <div><b>Deliver</b>One pull request per repo in scope, cross-linked. The work item gets every link, the ticket's page the cost.</div>
      </div>
      <p class="more"><a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/how-it-works/lifecycle/">Every step, and where the model gets a say</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ch" id="ticket">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <h2>Keep talking <em>on the ticket</em></h2>
        <p>When a ticket leaves a real decision open, Agent Smith stops and asks instead of guessing. The question shows up on the work item and on the ticket's page. Your answer resumes the run, or starts the next attempt after a hand-back, and no tokens burn while it waits.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ch-body flip">
        <div class="notes">
          <div><h3>Answer where you already are</h3><p>Reply in a work item comment in Azure Boards, or in the ticket's conversation in the dashboard. Both land in the same thread.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Every attempt is on the record</h3><p>The ticket's page lists what started each attempt, what it did and why it stopped, with the cost per spec and the time spent waiting on you.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Done means merged</h3><p>A ticket counts as done only when its pull request merged. Until then the page says who acts next.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="example-label">Example · a rate limit with an open question</div>
          <div class="app">
            <div class="app-bar"><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="path">Tickets · acme-platform · AB#1288</span><span class="tag person">waits for you</span></div>
            <div class="app-body">
              <div class="tp-head">
                <p class="tp-title">Rate-limit the public export endpoint</p>
                <div class="cells">
                  <div class="cell">attempts<b>2</b></div>
                  <div class="cell">specs<b>2 of 3</b></div>
                  <div class="cell">cost so far<b>$1.92</b></div>
                  <div class="cell">waiting on you<b>14 min</b></div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="tabs"><span class="on">Conversation</span><span>Specs</span><span>Acceptance</span><span>Attempts</span><span>Cost &amp; compute</span></div>
              <div class="handback">
                <div class="who">agent smith · attempt 1 handed back</div>
                <div class="bubble"><p>The ticket says "limit to 100 requests". Per minute per API key, or per client IP? <code class="c">ApiKeyMiddleware</code> already knows the key. Per IP would rely on the <code class="c">X-Forwarded-For</code> header the ingress sets.</p></div>
              </div>
              <div class="msg"><div class="av" aria-hidden="true">JM</div><div><div class="who">you · on the work item</div><div class="bubble"><p>Per API key, per minute. Return 429 with a Retry-After header.</p></div></div></div>
              <div class="msg"><div class="av av-smith" aria-hidden="true">AS</div><div><div class="who">agent smith</div><div class="bubble"><p>Taken as an acceptance criterion: <em>"over 100 requests per key per minute return 429 with Retry-After"</em>. Attempt 2 is running.</p></div></div></div>
              <div class="attempts">
                <div class="att"><span class="dot person"></span><span>Attempt 1 · stopped with a question</span><code>$0.84</code></div>
                <div class="att"><span class="dot working"></span><span>Attempt 2 · started by your reply · spec 2 of 3</span><code>$1.08</code></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ch" id="review">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <h2>Review it <em>like a colleague's</em> pull request</h2>
        <p>Write your comments in Azure Repos as you would for anyone. When you're done, vote Wait for author or comment <code class="c">/agent-smith</code>. The rework reads every open thread, changes the code, runs the checks again and pushes to the same branch.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ch-body">
        <div>
          <div class="example-label">Example · a reviewer asks for the shared retry helper</div>
          <div class="app">
            <div class="app-bar"><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="path">Azure Repos · acme-api · PR !412 · AB#1301</span><span class="tag working">rework running</span></div>
            <div class="app-body">
              <div class="diff">
                <div class="h">Webhooks/WebhookDispatcher.cs</div>
                <div class="del">-  for (var attempt = 0; attempt &lt; 5; attempt++)</div>
                <div class="del">-  {</div>
                <div class="del">-      if (await TrySendAsync(delivery, ct)) return;</div>
                <div class="del">-      await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow(2, attempt)), ct);</div>
                <div class="del">-  }</div>
                <div class="add">+  await _retry.ExecuteAsync(() =&gt; SendAsync(delivery, ct), ct);</div>
              </div>
              <div class="thread">
                <div class="msg"><div class="av" aria-hidden="true">MK</div><div><div class="who">reviewer · line 88</div><div class="bubble"><p>We already have <code class="c">RetryPolicy</code> in Acme.Common. Please use that instead of the hand-rolled loop. It also logs every attempt.</p></div></div></div>
                <div class="msg"><div class="av" aria-hidden="true">MK</div><div><div class="who">reviewer · line 41</div><div class="bubble"><p>The max delay should come from settings, not a constant.</p></div></div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="vote"><b>Wait for author</b><span>vote by the reviewer · starts the rework</span></div>
              <div class="msg"><div class="av av-smith" aria-hidden="true">AS</div><div><div class="who">agent smith · attempt 2</div><div class="bubble"><p>Replaced the loop with <code class="c">RetryPolicy.Exponential</code>, read the max delay from <code class="c">Webhooks:MaxRetryDelay</code>. Both threads resolved, all 11 criteria still met. Pushed <code class="c">3f9c2e1</code>.</p></div></div></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="notes">
          <div><h3>Batch your comments</h3><p>Comments alone start nothing. They are collected, and one vote or one <span class="cmd">/agent-smith</span> works through all of them together.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Only people who can push</h3><p>A command counts only from someone with Contribute permission on the repository. Anyone else costs no tokens and starts nothing.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Reviews on request</h3><p>Opening or pushing a pull request starts nothing. On anyone else's pull request, <span class="cmd">/as review</span> asks for a review. On Agent Smith's own, <span class="cmd">/as</span> starts a rework.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="more"><a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/reference/integrations/pr-comments/">Pull request commands and reworks</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ch" id="platforms">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">✶</div>
        <h2>Made for Azure DevOps. <em>At home</em> everywhere else.</h2>
        <p>Tickets come from the tracker you use. Pull requests go to the git host your code lives on. Mix them as your organisation already does.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="plat">
        <a class="pf azure" href="/azure-devops/">
          <div class="pair"><span class="tag person">Azure Boards</span><span class="tag person">Azure Repos</span></div>
          <h3>Azure DevOps</h3>
          <p>Work items in, pull requests out, without moving your code to another host.</p>
