      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="why">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">✶</div>
        <h2>Why we built it <em>this way</em></h2>
        <p>Agent Smith is created and maintained by {{ site.author }}. These are the choices behind the software factory, and the reasons for each.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Your code stays on your machine.</h3>
          <p>Agent Smith does not store any information about your code. It runs on your infrastructure and calls your AI provider directly. The more AI gets used, the more information tends to end up stored somewhere you never intended. Not here.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>You see what every run cost.</h3>
          <p>Every run records its token usage and its cost in dollars. The ticket's page splits it per attempt and per spec, next to the cap you set for the run, so you know what a ticket cost before you merge it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>The why gets written down.</h3>
          <p>Great developers write great code. They even write documentation. But we have never seen <a href="https://codingsoul.org/2026/04/11/the-why-never-gets-written-down/">the why being documented</a>. Agent Smith writes the specification it worked from next to the code and keeps a decision log in <code class="c">.agentsmith/decisions/</code>, each entry naming what it chose and why, so the reasoning stays available over the complete lifecycle of the program.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>You approve the spec, not a wall of diff.</h3>
          <p>Reviewing 800 changed lines tells you little about whether the right thing got built. So the moment to say yes or no is earlier: the specification. Approve it in a Work it out conversation and the run executes exactly that. Let it derive one from a plain ticket and the cut is on the ticket before any code exists, where a comment corrects it. Either way you judge the intent, and the delivery account judges the code against it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>One sandbox per repo, with its own toolchain.</h3>
          <p>You prefer mono repositories? Great, Agent Smith can work on UI, backend and workers directly. You got multi repositories per project? No problem. Agent Smith will create one sandbox per repository and create multiple pull requests.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Which languages are supported?</h3>
          <p>You write Python? The sandbox will be Python based. Dotnet? Sandbox will use your dotnet sdk. The language is not part of the Agent Smith, it is part of the infrastructure that is used to get the job done. There are no limitations in terms of languages.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="built">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">✶</div>
        <h2>Built by the method <em>it teaches</em></h2>
        <p>Agent Smith bootstraps an <code class="c">.agentsmith/</code> directory into your repo: context, specs, a decision log, a memory of what it learned. That is the product, and it is also how this repository got written, by Holger Leichsenring and one language model. About 750 hours of human time went in, roughly 37 minutes per completed spec.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figures">
        <div><b>1,229</b>completed specs</div>
        <div><b>699,551</b>lines of C#</div>
        <div><b>6,908</b>recorded decisions</div>
        <div><b>10,301</b>tests</div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>The tests do the steering</h3>
          <p>Ten coding principles are enforced as architecture tests: max 120 lines per file, state held in DI, exactly one method allowed to decide whether a run delivered, a gate that has to run <em>after</em> the thing it judges. Every one of them has something that went wrong behind it. When a rule was right and the existing code broke it 187 times, the violations went into a frozen baseline that entries can only leave, so the debt moves one direction and nobody has to schedule a cleanup week.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>The model has no way to wave itself through</h3>
          <p>A blocking commit hook lets a spec commit through once the dashboard's build and tests, the backend build, all 10,301 tests, four CLI dry runs and every pipeline harness preset come back green. CI would have told us about a break afterwards. The hook stops the commit from existing. And the <code class="c">principles.md</code> we built this project under is the same file Agent Smith injects into its own agents at runtime.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Including what didn't work</h3>
          <p>The skill catalog grew to 95 role definitions and then dropped to 12 in one breaking release, because stronger models did better with <code class="c">bash</code> and a clear goal than with narrow toolsets. The plan generator got retired after evaluations caught it truncating every multi repo plan. A cached token metric read zero for a long stretch while the caching underneath it worked fine. <a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/how-it-works/built-by-the-method/">The full account is in the docs</a>, along with how every figure here was counted.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="more">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ch-head">
        <div class="num" aria-hidden="true">✶</div>
        <h2>More pipelines <em>than the coding one</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-grid">
        <div>
          <p><code class="c">code</code> is the headline because it's the one most people show up for. It covers bug fixes, features, refactors and migrations alike. Seven more presets ship in the box: <code class="c">security-scan</code> and <code class="c">api-security-scan</code> (a security master reviewing a codebase or a live API), <code class="c">pr-review</code> (a review of a pull request, posted back as PR comments), <code class="c">legal-analysis</code> (contract review), <code class="c">mad-discussion</code> (multi-agent design discussion when you want to argue something out), <code class="c">init-project</code> (bootstraps the <code class="c">.agentsmith/</code> directory per component in each repo), and <code class="c">spec-dialog</code> (a design conversation that ends in a ticket or a spec draft). Same orchestrator, different masters, different output shapes.</p>
          <p>The masters and skills behind them live in a versioned catalog that ships inside the binary. You can pin another release, or layer your own skills on top without forking it. <a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/reference/pipelines/">Pipeline reference is in the docs.</a></p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Deploy as a single binary, Docker container, or Kubernetes. The sandbox-agent injects via an init-container into whatever toolchain image your repos use, so you get one carrier image plus the upstream toolchain image you already trust. Slack and Teams work as a front door too, for triggering runs and for the same design conversation the dashboard has.</p>
          <p><a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/connect-your-stuff/ai-providers/">AI providers</a> · <a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/host-it/docker-compose/">Docker Compose</a> · <a href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/host-it/kubernetes/">Kubernetes</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="end">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Have <span class="g">a look.</span></h2>
      <p><code class="c">agent-smith demo</code> runs the real pipeline against a bundled sample project. One model key, no tracker, no Docker.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-go" href="{{ site.docsUrl }}/get-it-running/install/">Get started</a>
